{
 "description": "Does a reversal that follows the worm's own speed ease the dwelling windows? (description)\n\nThe primitives give every reversal that starts on food one speed (0.069 mm/s, both states pooled),\nwhile real on-lawn reversals follow the speed just before them (`reversal_coupling_fit.json`:\nv_rev = 0.0474 + 0.576 x v_before, 0.049 to 0.084 mm/s across deciles). Here the evaluator is\npatched in each worker, with no package change: a reversal that starts on food moves at that line\napplied to the mean of the worm's last 3 run seconds, clipped to [0.049, 0.084] (the pooled value\nuntil 3 run seconds are seen). No draw changes, so each animal's random streams are the unpatched\nrun's. The third continuation's answer is graded with presence on the 10 populations of its own\ncheck (871,000,000 + 200\u00b7g + i), every statistic and mutant reading recorded, and set beside that\nunpatched check (`robustness_check_hinge3.json`) animal for animal.\n",
